## Claim
Text: "All USA Ballots Arriving After Election Day Will Be Thrown Out"
Claim author: Eric Holder
Claim date: October 28, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210216082556/https://twitter.com/EricHolder/status/1321123705200758786

## Initial Q&A
### Is there a single federal law in the United States that requires all ballots to be received by the close of polls on Election Day or else be discarded?
Answer: No, there is no single federal law requiring all ballots to be received by the close of polls on Election Day or else be discarded; as the article notes, "Deadlines for submitting mail ballots vary in other states," and the most common deadline is set by state law, not federal mandate.

Source URL: https://www.azcentral.com/story/news/politics/elections/2019/11/27/arizona-mail-ballot-deadline-lawsuit-filed-voto-latino-priorities-usa/4310966002/

### Did any U.S. state in the 2020 election have a law that explicitly allowed ballots to be counted if they were postmarked by Election Day but arrived after Election Day?
Answer: Yes, multiple U.S. states in the 2020 election had laws or court rulings that explicitly allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted if they arrived after Election Day. For example, North Carolina allowed ballots received within nine days after the election, Wisconsin within six days, Pennsylvania within three days, and Michigan within up to two weeks, as detailed in the NPR article.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/09/23/916012284/rule-changes-in-swing-states-mean-more-votes-will-count-results-may-take-longer

### Did any U.S. state in the 2020 election have a law that required ballots to be received by a specific date after Election Day (e.g., three days later) in order to be counted?
Answer: Yes, many U.S. states in the 2020 election had laws requiring mail-in ballots to be received by a specific date after Election Day. For example, the source lists deadlines such as "Postmarked on or before November 3, 2020 and received by November 8 by mail," "Postmarked on or before Election Day (Nov. 3) and received by Nov. 6, 2020 (12 p.m., EST)," and "Postmarked on or before Nov. 3, 2020 and received by Nov. 10, 2020." These are just a few instances where ballots had to be received anywhere from one to several days after the election.

Source URL: https://wildcatsports.com/sports/2020/9/22/voter-information.aspx

### Did the U.S. Supreme Court or any federal court issue a ruling in October 2020 that required all states to reject ballots that arrived after Election Day?
Answer: No, neither the U.S. Supreme Court nor any federal court issued a ruling in October 2020 that required all states to reject ballots arriving after Election Day. In fact, the Supreme Court issued a 4-4 tie that allowed Pennsylvania to count ballots received up to three days after Election Day, as long as they were not clearly postmarked after Election Day.

Source URL: https://www.naco.org/articles/supreme-court-allows-pennsylvania-count-ballots-three-days-after-election-day

### Did any state, like Pennsylvania, have a state supreme court ruling in 2020 that extended the ballot receipt deadline beyond Election Day due to the COVID-19 pandemic?
Answer: Yes, Pennsylvania’s state supreme court ruled in September 2020 that mail-in ballots could be accepted until 5 p.m. on November 6 (three days after Election Day), provided they were postmarked by November 3, citing the COVID-19 pandemic and anticipated postal delays. The source notes: “the state Supreme Court ruled officials could accept ballots until 5 p.m. on Nov. 6.”

Source URL: https://www.spotlightpa.org/news/2020/10/pa-mail-ballots-republican-voters-donald-trump/

### Did Eric Holder's statement on October 28, 2020, specifically refer to a particular state's election law, or did it claim to describe a nationwide rule?
Answer: The statement "All USA Ballots Arriving After Election Day Will Be Thrown Out" explicitly refers to "USA" ballots, indicating it claims to describe a nationwide rule rather than a particular state's election law.

Source URL: https://www.plannedparenthood.org/planned-parenthood-pacific-southwest/blog/voting-by-mail

### For the 2020 U.S. general election, did all 50 states and the District of Columbia have identical laws regarding the deadline for receiving and counting absentee or mail-in ballots?
Answer: No, the laws were not identical; the search result explicitly states that "some states ... have said mail-in ballots only need to be postmarked by Election Day, rather than received by then," indicating variation in deadlines across states.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/09/14/909338758/map-mail-in-voting-rules-by-state

### In the context of U.S. election law, does the phrase "arriving after Election Day" apply to ballots that are postmarked before Election Day but are delivered by the U.S. Postal Service after Election Day, and does the rule differ by state?
Answer: Yes, the rule differs by state. The search result notes that "a number of states only require ballots to be postmarked by Nov. 3," meaning that ballots postmarked before or on Election Day but delivered afterward are still valid in those states, while other states require ballots to be received by Election Day. Thus, whether "arriving after Election Day" applies depends on each state's specific law.

Source URL: https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/why-were-planning-for-an-election-day-that-could-last-months/

## Final Judgement
The key insight from the fact-check is that there is no single federal law mandating that all ballots arriving after Election Day must be discarded; instead, ballot receipt deadlines vary by state. Multiple states in the 2020 election explicitly allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted if they arrived afterward (e.g., North Carolina, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Michigan), and the U.S. Supreme Court upheld Pennsylvania’s extension. The claim, which refers to "All USA Ballots," asserts a nationwide rule that is contradicted by the evidence showing that many states do not throw out such ballots. Therefore, the claim is clearly false.

