### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check found no single federal law requiring all ballots arriving after Election Day to be discarded; instead, ballot receipt deadlines vary by state. Multiple states in the 2020 election explicitly allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted if they arrived afterward (e.g., North Carolina, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, Michigan)[source](https://www.npr.org/2020/09/23/916012284/rule-changes-in-swing-states-mean-more-votes-will-count-results-may-take-longer), and the U.S. Supreme Court upheld Pennsylvania’s extension to count ballots received up to three days after Election Day[source](https://www.naco.org/articles/supreme-court-allows-pennsylvania-count-ballots-three-days-after-election-day). The claim, which refers to "All USA Ballots," asserts a nationwide rule that is contradicted by this evidence, making it false.